// TEST: One let-bound lambda used with an array and with a function
// ============================================================================
// Was functions/219_..._rejects: a lambda was ONE emitted body, so its first
// use decided its arrow parameter was an array and the function argument was
// refused (BL3001). A top-level `let` of a lambda that applies an unannotated
// parameter, used only by APPLICATION, is now checked as the `function`
// declaration it is equivalent to (TypeCheck.generalizeArrowLetLambdas): the
// parameter is generic, each call decides its kind, and one body is emitted
// per kind (functions/213), in both lanes.
let u = lambda(c) -> c(0) + 1.0
let w = u([5.0, 6.0])
// EXPECT: w = 6
let w2 = u(lambda(i) -> Float64(i))
// EXPECT: w2 = 1
// A third call, an array of another length: the array instance again.
let w3 = u([7.0, 8.0, 9.0])
// EXPECT: w3 = 8
